bit_cast between signed and unsigned vectors for the raw_buffer builtins.

clang 23 changed __builtin_amdgcn_raw_buffer_load_b{32,64,128} to RETURN, and
__builtin_amdgcn_raw_buffer_store_b128 to TAKE, unsigned vector types. CK
declares the corresponding values as signed int32 vectors, so the build fails
both ways:

    error: cannot initialize a variable of type 'int32x2_t' ... with an rvalue
    of type 'unsigned int __attribute__((ext_vector_type(2)))'
    error: cannot initialize a parameter of type 'unsigned int
    __attribute__((ext_vector_type(4)))' ... with an lvalue of type
    'const int __attribute__((ext_vector_type(4)))'

bit_cast at each call site keeps the declared types intact so surrounding code
-- which stores these into vector_type<int32_t, N> slots via AsType<int32x4_t>
-- is untouched. (Deducing loads with `auto` instead compiles the declarations
but just relocates the same error onto those assignments.) Reinterpreting the
bits is what the adjacent bit_cast<int8xN_t> calls already do, so there is no
change in value semantics.

Only the widths that actually changed are touched: the b8/b16 stores and the
b8/b16 loads still use matching types and are left alone.

Note this is a vanilla-LLVM issue: AMD builds ROCm against its own LLVM fork,
where these builtins kept the signed signatures.
verified 2026-08-29 against therock-10.0 with clang 23.1.0.
--- a/include/ck/utility/amd_buffer_addressing_builtins.hpp
+++ b/include/ck/utility/amd_buffer_addressing_builtins.hpp
